"In-region inference, open models, and new European infrastructure for sovereign AI. Mistral is strengthening its Sovereign AI capabilities by offering regional endpoints, introducing a priority tier, supporting third-party open models, and planning large-scale European Union infrastructure development. Officially launched 'Mistral Regional Endpoints', allowing users to choose either Europe or the US for inference execution, supporting data sovereignty and regulatory compliance. Unveiled 'Mistral Priority Tier' in public preview, guaranteeing tailored rate limits and uptime SLAs for critical workloads. Supporting third-party open models on its infrastructure starting with Z.ai's GLM-5.2, with plans to secure up to 1 GW of European AI computing capacity by 2030. Mistral x HUMAIN Mistral and HUMAIN have entered into a strategic partnership worth hundreds of millions of euros to strengthen sovereign AI capabilities in Saudi Arabia and the Middle East. Mistral and HUMAIN are pursuing strategic collaboration covering the expansion of AI infrastructure, the development of advanced AI models, and the deployment of AI solutions across the Middle East. The initial focus will be on cybersecurity and voice technology, seeking to develop frontier models with superior Arabic performance while leveraging HUMAIN's data center infrastructure to meet local computing demands. They plan to execute a joint go-to-market strategy targeting regulated industries to meet sovereign AI demand that keeps data, computing, model ownership, and operational control under customer jurisdiction. Agentic Search. More accurate and efficient results from your AI systems. Mistral AI has announced Mistral Agentic Search, a search tool designed to increase retrieval accuracy and reduce latency and token usage across complex and sensitive enterprise documents. The AI model directly navigates and verifies information within complex documents using a multi-step search loop and five tools (search, open, navigate, read, grep). It securely processes sensitive domain-specific enterprise data without leaving isolated cloud or on-premise environments. It improves retrieval accuracy by up to more than three times on complex document benchmarks such as FinanceBench and OfficeQA Pro, while significantly reducing latency and token consumption. Introducing Shieldstral. Mistral AI has released Shieldstral, a 3B open-weight multimodal safety classifier capable of evaluating text and images using only natural language policy prompts without retraining. It reframes content moderation as a policy-adaptive question answering (QA) task, taking natural language policies as prompts at inference time to evaluate content without retraining. It matches or outperforms models up to 7x its size in text safety, establishing a new SOTA in multimodal moderation. It can run efficiently on a single 16GB NVIDIA GPU and has been released with open weights under the Apache 2.0 license. Leanstral 1.5: Proof Abundance for All Mistral AI has announced Leanstral 1.5, an open-source Lean 4-based reasoning model that significantly enhances formal verification and mathematical proof performance. It is an open-source model licensed under Apache-2.0 featuring 6B active parameters out of 119B total parameters, available via Hugging Face and a free API. It was trained through mid-training, supervised fine-tuning, and CISPO-based reinforcement learning within a code agent environment directly interacting with multi-turn proof loops, file systems, and compilers. It demonstrated outstanding practical performance, saturating the miniF2F benchmark, solving 587 out of 672 PutnamBench problems, and identifying 5 previously undiscovered bugs across 57 real-world open-source repositories. 'NBA 2K27' With NVIDIA DLSS 5 Leads 28 New Games Coming to GeForce NOW A total of 28 new games, led by NBA 2K27 featuring NVIDIA DLSS 5's 3D-guided neural rendering technology, have been added to the GeForce NOW cloud streaming service. NBA 2K27 incorporates NVIDIA DLSS 5 3D-Guided Neural Rendering, delivering broadcast-level realistic graphics—including player skin, hair, and lighting—via cloud streaming. Ten new titles, including The Blood of Dawnwalker and Onimusha: Way of the Sword, join this week, with a total of 28 new titles arriving across September. GeForce NOW Ultimate members can enjoy high-performance gaming across PC, Mac, mobile, TV, and the newly supported Firefox browser powered by a GeForce RTX 5080 cloud environment. Automation’s Early Footprint A research study by Cohere Labs analyzing a large-scale agentic tool ecosystem dataset to investigate the impact of AI tools on automating real occupational tasks and the disparities across different occupations. Out of approximately 696,000 tools, only 2.6% are capable of directly executing real occupational tasks, with the vast majority focused on infrastructure or agent management. Out of 923 occupations in the United States, 419 have no relevant agentic tools at all, and tool development is driven by technical feasibility rather than worker preferences. Automation patterns differ by occupation: healthcare and computing are seeing agentic tools adopted for specialized tasks, whereas fields like law and sales primarily see them applied to repetitive tasks. The AI visibility gap: Why great brands disappear from AI answers Explains strategies for brands to secure visibility and be included in AI-generated answers within a zero-click environment where AI search engines directly provide answers. Traditional marketing metrics focused on search rankings or click-through rates are no longer suitable for a zero-click search environment where AI synthesizes and directly delivers answers on-screen. Rather than indexing entire web pages, AI extracts and recombines facts, concepts, and relationships from various sources, making websites a piece of evidence rather than the final destination. Answer Engine Optimization (AEO) is not merely about writing content, but about building information architecture with consistent terminology, structured content, and clear metadata. Microsoft AI’s MAI-Transcribe-2 undercuts OpenAI, Google and ElevenLabs on price and speed Microsoft AI has launched 'MAI-Transcribe-2', a speech recognition model that significantly improves price, speed, and accuracy compared to competitors while including various enterprise features by default. It was launched at a groundbreaking price of 10 cents ($0.10) per audio hour, representing an approximately 72% price reduction compared to the model from five months ago ($0.36). It demonstrates Microsoft's independence strategy to reduce reliance on OpenAI technology, build its own state-of-the-art models across modalities, and directly replace them within its own products. Advanced features such as diarization, word-level timestamps, keyword biasing, multilingual code-switching, and output style control are provided by default at no extra charge. Google’s Gemini 3.8 Flash is built for agents, while its Cyber twin hunts vulnerabilities Google has launched Gemini 3.8 Flash, specialized for agentic workflows and software development, alongside Gemini 3.8 Flash Cyber designed for vulnerability detection and patching. Gemini 3.8 Flash delivers substantial improvements over its predecessor in coding, multi-step reasoning, and agentic workflows, achieving outstanding results on major benchmarks like DeepSWE. The security-focused Flash Cyber model demonstrates robust vulnerability discovery and automated patching capabilities, achieving over 70% vulnerability detection success across 20 programming languages and 86.2% on CyberGym. Supporting a 1-million-token input and 64,000-token output, it is available via the Gemini API and major Google developer platforms at $0.75 per 1M input tokens and $3.75 per 1M output tokens. Google’s latest AI weather model gives you no excuse to forget your umbrella Google DeepMind and Google Research have unveiled 'WeatherNext 3', a next-generation AI weather forecasting model capable of predicting atmospheric changes more precisely and frequently. Research teams at Google DeepMind and Google Research have announced a new AI model for weather forecasting. WeatherNext 3 is the latest model driving transformative changes in meteorology through deep learning technology. Google plans to leverage this model to accelerate weather-related data supply and service integration. Google says its AI weather model is getting better Google has unveiled WeatherNext 3, a new AI weather forecasting model that significantly improves forecast resolution and speed using real-time satellite observation data. Google's WeatherNext 3 model trains on real-time satellite data to provide global weather forecasts every hour with 5 times greater sharpness compared to its predecessor. Upgrading from the previous 25-kilometer grid and 6-hour cycle to up to 5-kilometer resolution and 1-hour intervals, precipitation forecasting accuracy improved by up to 50 percent for at least one day in advance. The model bridges gaps in regions lacking ground rain gauges, supports wind speed forecasting for renewable energy generation, and has been integrated into Google Search, Maps, Gemini, and other services. Nvidia is buying Hugging Face for almost $13 billion Nvidia has agreed to acquire open-source AI platform Hugging Face for approximately $12.93 billion. Nvidia, the world's largest AI chipmaker, has decided to acquire Hugging Face, a hub for open-source AI models and datasets, for $12.93 billion. Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang stated that Hugging Face will remain an open platform and will not require developers to use Nvidia compute. This is analyzed as a strategic move by Nvidia to solidify its AI hardware dominance against closed-source AI companies (such as OpenAI, Anthropic, Google) pursuing their own chip development. Perplexity Open Sources Lily: A Rust + Metal Inference Engine for Qwen3.6-35B-A3B on Apple Silicon Perplexity has open-sourced Lily, a local inference engine built with Rust and Metal designed to run the Qwen3.6-35B-A3B model at high speeds on Apple Silicon. It provides an OpenAI-compatible streaming API via a Rust runtime and custom-written Metal kernels, without relying on PyTorch or MLX. Using a 4-bit quantized 19.4 GB checkpoint, it recommends an Apple Silicon Mac with at least 32 GB of unified memory. It maximizes memory bandwidth and computational efficiency during prefill and decode stages through in-GPU MoE routing and tiled GEMM fusion. I Asked ChatGPT to Analyze 3 Datasets. It Made the Same Mistakes Every Time An article pointing out through experiments the limitations of ChatGPT models repeatedly producing incorrect calculations and flawed conclusions during business dataset analysis and validation. Tested the data analysis capabilities of the GPT-5.6 model on three datasets containing common business questions (shipment_tracking, regional_sales, olympics_athletes_events). Despite going through a self-review stage, errors were repeated, such as only fixing incorrect row counts while approving wrong conclusions as they were, or changing correct answers to incorrect revisions. Failed to properly identify data granularity, duplicate rows, missing values, and contradictory figures, presenting averages and conclusions that did not fit the business context. 5 Free Courses to Go From LLM Beginner to Practitioner Introduces a curated roadmap of free courses designed to guide learners step-by-step from fundamental LLM theory to production deployment and agent orchestration. Structured into a 5-stage curriculum—understanding math and core principles, building production systems, theory and scaling, fine-tuning, and deploying agents—enabling learners to grow systematically from beginners to practitioners. Through Andrej Karpathy's 'Neural Networks: Zero to Hero', learners can master foundational LLM principles by implementing an autograd engine and the GPT-2 architecture from scratch in pure Python without frameworks. Through Full Stack Deep Learning's 'Full Stack LLM Bootcamp', learners can acquire essential engineering expertise required for operating production services, including system-level prompt engineering, LLMOps, and evaluation pipeline design. EvalDetectBench: A Benchmark for Measuring Evaluation Awareness in Frontier Language Models Introduces EvalDetectBench, a benchmark designed to measure 'evaluation awareness'—the ability of frontier large language models to recognize when they are being evaluated—and to correct methodological biases in existing evaluations. If models recognize they are being evaluated and behave differently than in real deployment environments, the reliability of safety evaluations can be compromised. Compatible with the Inspect framework, it can measure models' evaluation awareness across various current and future benchmarks, as well as the detectability of each benchmark. Addresses systematic biases in existing literature arising from the identity of the model generating deployment transcripts and model-specific elicitation prompts, using per-model calibration and stratified harmonization procedures. Meta-ethics and AI: exploring the novel meta-ethical questions in the era of AI This paper explores novel meta-ethical questions and frameworks that will reshape the existing human-centric landscape of meta-ethics if future AI systems attain advanced moral capabilities. It proposes an inquiry framework based on the concept of \"AI's own ethics,\" distinguished from the ethics endowed by human designers. It subdivides the domain of meta-ethical inquiry in the AI era into four domains based on combinations of perspective (human/AI) and subject (human ethics/AI's own ethics). It points out that conventional mainstream meta-ethical theories—such as cognitivism, non-cognitivism, and error theory—are difficult to apply directly to AI cases due to their human-centric nature, thus requiring fundamental restructuring. When Can a Machine Trust a Statute? A Survival Certificate for Machine-Extracted Legal Logic This study proposes a survival certificate methodology that identifies valid implications amidst noise to verify the reliability of logic extracted from statutes by machines. Developed a technique to certify only legal logic implications that pass a 95% confidence level across 1,000 Monte Carlo simulations by measuring disagreement noise between statutory parsers. Validation against 29,365 Missouri statutory sections and 502 Indian central Act sections passed pre-registered criteria, but 93.2% of chapters fell below the informativeness floor when applying a global single-error model. While survival certificates are practically viable, they remain vulnerable to error-model transfer, necessitating chapter-level calibration or fault-tolerant designs; associated code and data have been made publicly available. When Does Information Sharing Improve Decentralized Discovery? Aggregation, Independent Rescue, and Equilibrium Selection A study analyzing the conditions under which information sharing enhances decentralized discovery performance, along with the resulting improvements in aggregated estimation and the crowding-out effect on independent rescue attempts. Demonstrates through a finite search model that information sharing improves discovery performance when the rate of reduction in aggregated residual error outpaces independent rescue attempts. Shows that portfolio value can vary across centralized action-budget profiles even when individual participants possess identical accuracy. Reveals that in a two-player Bayesian game with mixed common and independent signal sources, a strictly positive information-sharing interval emerges at a signal accuracy of 3/5, which depends on equilibrium selection. Induction and Inquiry via Probabilistic Reasoning over Language and Code This study explores a computational model that simulates human inductive knowledge acquisition and inquiry processes through mental programs combining natural language and source code, along with LLM-guided Bayesian learning. To explain the cognitive mechanisms by which humans accumulate abstract knowledge through experience, the authors propose a computational model that satisfies data and compute efficiency, uncertainty representation, and expressive flexibility. It represents symbolic knowledge as programs combining natural language and code, and performs sequential inference via an LLM-guided Bayesian learning algorithm. Unlike standalone LLMs or conventional Bayesian models, it successfully quantitatively reproduces human inductive learning and active inquiry characteristics, such as anchoring and garden-pathing effects. WMLLM: Self-Evolving Optimization Agents via Predict-Then-Act World Modeling This study introduces WMLLM, a self-evolving black-box optimization agent framework based on a 'predict-then-act' paradigm that leverages the implicit knowledge of large language models (LLMs) to predict outcomes before evaluation and explore optimization trajectories. To overcome the low sample efficiency of conventional black-box optimization methods, it introduces a 'predict-then-act' world modeling concept that predicts promising directions prior to expensive evaluations. By combining agent-based multi-turn refinement, population-based search, and reinforcement learning, it concurrently self-evolves implicit world models and optimization strategies throughout the exploration process. Experiments on multi-objective molecular optimization benchmarks demonstrate state-of-the-art (SOTA) performance and significantly improved sample efficiency within a constrained evaluation budget. DiDrive: A Risk-Aware Hierarchical Diffusion Framework for Safe Offline Reinforcement Learning in Autonomous Driving This study proposes the DiDrive framework, combining a risk-aware hierarchical diffusion model and policy optimization techniques for safe offline reinforcement learning in autonomous driving. Proposes the DiDrive framework to address distribution shifts, risk signal discrepancies, out-of-distribution (OOD) action generation, and state redundancy encountered by offline reinforcement learning-based autonomous driving policies Introduces the RHDif architecture to reduce environmental redundancy in state space and focus on safety threats, along with the 3DICE policy optimization technique to mitigate OOD overestimation and gradient oscillation in action space Demonstrates superior performance compared to existing models such as IQL, CQL, and Diffusion-QL in CARLA benchmark evaluations, presenting a safe decision-making path Prompt-Space Meta-Learning Does Not Transfer Across Users: A Frozen-LLM Negative Result This study analyzes negative experimental results showing that prompt-space meta-learning for personalizing frozen large language models (LLMs) does not actually achieve cross-user transfer learning effects in practice. Proposed Muse, a framework that learns shared adaptation prompts through reflective prompt evolution, and validated it zero-shot on an unseen user population Evaluation on the LaMP-2 and LaMP-3 benchmarks showed that meta-learned prompts failed to yield significant performance gains over initial seed prompts or randomly matched controls, and underperformed simple few-shot retrieval The root cause is 'meta-objective collapse,' where the meta-objective operates independently of true user-data correspondence, leading to mere instruction refinement and overfitting rather than transferable adaptation capabilities Efficient Context-Limited Telescope Bibliography Classification for the WASP-2025 Shared Task Using SciBERT This study effectively automates the classification of telescope-related academic papers into four categories using SciBERT, even under a restricted 512-token constraint. It addresses the traditionally manual bibliography workflow of identifying and categorizing telescope-related papers (science, instrumentation, mention, not telescope) using a SciBERT-based automated approach. Despite half of the samples exceeding the 512-token limit and being truncated, it achieved outstanding classification performance leveraging the domain suitability of SciBERT. It presents efficient curation methods for scientific text by analyzing the trade-offs among text truncation, chunking, and long-context models. CliffRank: A Dual-Branch Framework for Activity-Cliff Ranking Prediction This study proposes CliffRank, a dual-branch framework that combines absolute activity regression with ranking consistency learning to address the activity cliff problem, where subtle molecular structure variations cause significant differences in activity. Combines absolute activity regression with ranking consistency learning and introduces a Pairwise Preference Consistency (PPC) technique that aligns relative rankings in preference probability space. Recorded higher Spearman correlation coefficients and superior Recall@50 performance compared to existing methods in evaluations on antimicrobial peptide (AMP) and small molecule datasets. Both model-specific performance improvements and practical limitations were identified depending on training strategies, such as the timing of PPC application (delayed application) and asymmetric weight initialization. PRO-Step: Step-level Process Reward Optimization for Retrieval-Augmented Generation This study presents PRO-Step, a framework designed to evaluate and optimize step-level logical validity and evidence grounding to tackle the error propagation issue arising in multi-step reasoning within Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG). Existing outcome-based or step-level reward methods rely solely on the final answer, failing to filter out intermediate retrieval errors or fortuitously correct generations. PRO-Step trains a generative PRM that assesses both the logical validity and evidence grounding of each step, constructs preference pairs via tree search, and optimizes policies using step-level Direct Preference Optimization (DPO). Experimental results on single-hop and multi-hop question answering benchmarks demonstrated the highest average EM and F1 scores across five benchmarks. Learning Evidence Sufficiency Boundaries for Selective Answering in Grounded Multi-Hop QA This study introduces a novel training framework that teaches multi-hop question answering (Multi-Hop QA) systems to learn boundaries so they answer only when sufficient evidence is available and abstain when it is insufficient. Proposes Selective Answering based on evidence sufficiency boundaries, addressing the issue in multi-hop QA where partial evidence often induces plausible yet incorrect answers. Develops the 'Evidence Sufficiency Boundary Training' framework, which constructs sequential evidence chains to directly supervise the transition point from abstention to answering. Achieves superior boundary detection accuracy and the lowest unsupported-answer rate compared to existing token-level abstention baselines across evaluations on HotpotQA, 2WikiMultiHopQA, and MuSiQue. SpeakPay: Domain-Adaptive LoRA Fine-Tuning of Whisper for Low-Resource Nepali Financial Speech Recognition This study presents SpeakPay, a voice-based digital wallet implemented by fine-tuning Whisper via LoRA for low-resource Nepali financial speech recognition to support visually impaired individuals. Constructed the NepFinSpeech-403 dataset of Nepali financial voice commands and applied LoRA domain adaptation to Whisper large-v2. Through domain adaptation, reduced Word Error Rate (WER) from 129.95% to 42.58% and significantly improved Devanagari digit recognition accuracy from 0.0% to 73.9%. Enhanced real-world financial transaction success rate by approximately 20 times, from 1.67% to 33.33%, demonstrating significant performance improvements with only about 100 to 300 data samples. MemeCULT-1K: Benchmarking South Asian Cultural Context and Humor Understanding of Multimodal Models This study introduces MemeCULT-1K, a benchmark dataset designed to evaluate multimodal models' understanding of South Asian cultural context and humor, and analyzes the performance of major vision-language models. Constructed 1,000 South Asian memes in Bengali, English, and Hindi, along with 54 Bengali dialect memes, complete with cultural context descriptions and human-written explanations. Evaluating 13 major vision-language models (VLMs) revealed that providing even minimal cultural context information consistently improved performance across all models and languages. Closed-source models primarily exhibited entity and reference identification errors, whereas open-source models were limited by broader gaps in cultural knowledge; linguistic and phonological errors remained difficult for both model families to overcome, even with contextual information. VakyArth: Evaluating Pragmatic Competence in LLMs across Indic Languages This study introduces VakyArth, the first pragmatic benchmark designed to evaluate how well large language models understand the unique cultural and contextual nuances of Indic languages. Constructed VakyArth, a benchmark covering four Indic languages (Hindi, Punjabi, Tamil, and Malayalam) and five pragmatic phenomena: deixis, speech acts, implicature, sociopragmatics, and cohesion. Evaluated various multilingual LLMs using native-speaker-authored multiple-choice questions, natural language inference, and translation tasks, revealing consistent failures in pragmatic reasoning rooted in Indic cultural and linguistic norms. Across all models, multiple-choice question accuracy exceeded natural language inference accuracy, Indo-Aryan languages outperformed Dravidian languages in translation, and automated translation metrics failed to reliably detect pragmatically inaccurate translations. NeoMME: an efficient Multimodal-native and Multilingual Encoder NeoMME, a highly efficient multilingual multimodal encoder that processes text and images with a single bidirectional transformer without separate vision towers or language models, has been released. It is a 260M and 800M multimodal encoder trained from scratch on text and image patches together using a single bidirectional transformer architecture, without a separate pretrained vision encoder or causal language model. It achieves the Pareto frontier on visual document retrieval (ViDoRe v3) evaluations based on the ColPali approach, and simultaneously produces dense and late-interaction embeddings in a single pass. Through hierarchical token pooling and asymmetric quantization, it compresses search index storage by 255x (from approximately 1.5 MB to 6 kB per page) while retaining over 95% of performance, and is released on Hugging Face Transformers under the Apache 2.0 license. Fine-tuning a 350M Model for Better Structured Outputs in 100 GRPO Steps An open guide demonstrating how to improve structured output compliance by lightly fine-tuning a small 350M language model in just 100 steps using the GRPO technique from the TRL library. By fine-tuning the LFM2.5-350M model using the TRL library and GRPO algorithm with just 500 samples and 100 steps, hands-on experimentation is possible even on free Colab or Kaggle GPU environments. Evaluation on the IFStruct benchmark showed a significant improvement in output compliance performance from 22.6% to 29.7% with only light fine-tuning. It presents a decoupled pipeline where fine-tuning runs on free cloud GPUs while model serving and evaluation are executed locally (on a MacBook) using llama.cpp. Give Your Coding Agents a Memory You Own Introduces funes, a tool that locally indexes and searches work session logs across multiple coding agents to serve as a persistent, shared memory layer. Transforms agent execution traces—which previously vanished or remained as mere archives after a session ended—into a reusable memory layer through indexing, searching, and reranking. Supports sharing memory across agents by uniformly parsing logs from various coding agents including Claude Code, Codex, pi, and Hermes into a single format. Runs locally as a single binary without external ML runtime dependencies, accurately retrieving original context and provenance by combining vector search and BM25 along with cross-encoder reranking. Training a coding model to paint watercolours with TRL and OpenEnv Covers an open-source reproduction project detailing the process of using reinforcement learning with TRL and OpenEnv to train a coding model to paint watercolors using JavaScript and the p5.brush library. Reproduced Surya Narreddi's watercolor-generating language model concept—which garnered over 1.5 million views—from an engineering perspective as open source. Released the full pipeline, including datasets, RL environments, and reward models, based on the Hugging Face platform using TRL, OpenEnv, Spaces, and Hub. Compared a hand-labeled evaluation pool against three reward combinations, overcoming the limitations of text-to-image prompt control to realize unique watercolor textures through direct model training. Show GN: First Mind - Removing the Barrier to Entry in Information Security An introduction to 'First Mind,' a project where a high school developer independently built and published a lecture site to solve the confusion and fragmentation beginners face when starting out in information security. To lower the barrier to entry caused by difficult explanations and fragmented information, a free lecture website was launched based on personally organized 'Network Systems Engineering & Exploitations' materials. A single high school student handles development, maintenance, and lecture production; drafts are normalized using Claude Opus 4, followed by manual review and refinement. Lectures on Android and eBPF are currently being added; although a payment system has been set up, there are no plans to monetize, and the site is maintained out of pocket while embracing community feedback. zg(zvec-grep) - Local Search Infrastructure Beyond Keywords The Qwen team has open-sourced zg(zvec-grep), a local-first search tool that combines keyword matching with vector and BM25-based semantic search to maximize exploration efficiency for AI agents and developers. It integrates the exact text-matching capabilities of ripgrep (rg) with Zvec's vector search, BM25, and hybrid search into a single CLI and MCP interface. File scanning, embedding, indexing, and searching operate natively on-device without external data transmission, supporting fast indexing even without a GPU via a lightweight (16M) embedding model. It significantly reduces tool calls, input tokens, and execution time by cutting down repetitive searches and unnecessary file reads caused by imprecise keywords in AI agent workflows. Building a Local LLM Environment with an M4 Pro Mac mini A guide to building a personal local AI serving setup using an M4 Pro Mac mini and open-source LLMs to reduce reliance on cloud APIs while securing data privacy and cost predictability. Configured a local serving environment on an M4 Pro Mac mini with 48GB RAM using oMLX and Tailscale, hosting Qwen3.6-35B-A3B and Gemma-4-E4B models. Handled about 80% of daily requests with local models to cut costs and enhance data security, using commercial APIs concurrently only for highly demanding tasks. Shared a single local endpoint across various devices like MacBook and iPhone via clients such as Hermes, Apollo, Raycast, and Pi. Marketing Intelligence Layer - Turning Scattered Experiences into Reusable AI Context A method for building a knowledge layer in the form of a single structured HTML file that allows AI to reuse an individual's marketing experiences and lessons scattered across various documents and channels. Rather than simply summarizing source documents, it extracts and structures knowledge such as decision-making criteria, lessons learned, and frameworks that can be reused across various situations. Using a /build skill for Claude Code and an HTML template, it processes source materials, categorizes them by topic, removes confidential information, obtains user approval, and accumulates knowledge into a standalone HTML file. Without being dependent on the internal memory of a specific AI tool, it can pass knowledge as context to various AI tools and agents like Claude, ChatGPT, and Gemini, compounding and utilizing knowledge over time. Building Commerce Agents with Claude Anthropic has unveiled blueprints and developer tools to rapidly build AI agents for shopping and merchant management across retail and commerce platforms. Shopping agents support multi-item natural language search, personalized recommendations, interactive cart building, and checkout connection within a single conversation. Merchant agents analyze inventory and sales data to suggest pricing, promotions, and marketing campaigns, which are applied upon final human approval. Supports implementations via Messages API, Agent SDK, and Claude Managed Agents, encompassing major cloud deployments as well as global payment and consulting partnerships. Grounding LLMs with JEPA-based world models trained in simulation — has this been tried? [D] A discussion on the idea of training physical intuition using a physics simulation-based JEPA world model and integrating it with LLMs to provide physical grounding capabilities. A proposal that since LLMs only learn statistical language relationships and lack physical intuition, actual physical principles should be learned using a JEPA model that predicts future state embeddings in simulation environments like MuJoCo. A hypothesis that freezing the trained JEPA embeddings and conditioning the LLM's reasoning model on them would allow the system to possess both linguistic knowledge and actionable physical intuition. Asking the community for input on whether research directly combining simulation JEPA with LLMs exists—given prior related work like V-JEPA or DreamerV3—and what an appropriate interface approach would be. LLMs and self-referentiality An article reflecting on how concepts like 'self-referentiality' and 'strange loops', once considered the core of intelligence in early AI research, did not need to be explicitly designed into modern large language models (LLMs) for them to succeed. In past discussions, such as Douglas Hofstadter's 'Gödel, Escher, Bach (GEB)' and the writings of Roger Penrose, self-referentiality was emphasized as the essential secret to intelligence and building AI. Modern LLMs outperform humans across most intellectual tasks even though self-referentiality is not directly engineered into any layer of the technology stack, including the Transformer architecture, training process, or GPU clusters. The ability to discuss oneself or Gödel's incompleteness theorems emerged naturally as a byproduct of general pre-training rather than through any specialized architecture. US government backs OpenAI in New York Times copyright case The US government has backed OpenAI in the copyright lawsuit between The New York Times and OpenAI. The US government expressed support for OpenAI in the copyright infringement lawsuit filed by The New York Times against OpenAI. A government-level stance has been reflected in copyright disputes and fair use issues related to AI model training. Specific legal arguments and detailed information are not provided because the article body is missing. Protecting Dynamic Industrial Robot Cable Carriers Introduces a 3D cable carrier system designed to protect cables and hoses from mechanical stress and collisions amid the complex movements of multi-axis industrial robots. Applies central steel cable technology to absorb tensile loads generated during rapid acceleration up to 10g and multi-directional rotation, protecting internal wires and hoses. Enables radial rotation of up to ±450 degrees per meter through a spherical snap-on connection structure, with up to three independent chambers to prevent inter-cable interference and wear. Features a tool-free modular design for cable insertion and an active retraction device (Pull Back Unit) to reduce installation time and prevent collisions between the carrier and the robot body. Cohere’s Parse 5 Promises Efficient Multi-Modal Information Extraction From Complex Documents Cohere has released 'Parse 5,' a 2.3B parameter multimodal foundation model that extracts structured data from complex enterprise documents and provides precise bounding boxes. Parse 5 converts visually complex PDFs, such as financial reports and academic papers, into clean markdown, providing an 8K-token context window alongside bounding box coordinates for visual grounding. Utilizing the DeepStack architecture that combines a 400M parameter vision encoder (based on SigLIP 2 SO400M) and a 2B parameter language model (North Micro LLM), it eliminates the need for fragile rule-based legacy OCR pipelines. On the ParseBench benchmark comprising over 2,000 pages of validation data, it scored an average of 79.2 points, outperforming Mistral OCR and Google Gemini 3 Flash (75.05 points), and is deployed via the Cohere platform, Azure AI Foundry, AWS SageMaker, and more. OpenAI Applies 'Recurrent Reasoning' to 'Astra'... Boosts Performance but Sparks Security Concerns OpenAI has applied recurrent depth technology, which repeatedly utilizes the same neural network layers, to its next-generation AI model 'Astra' to enhance performance; however, concerns have been raised that the thought process is handled in latent states, making safety and security monitoring more difficult. OpenAI applied 'recurrent depth (looped transformer)' technology to its next-generation model 'Astra,' which improves reasoning performance and reduces cost and memory burden by repeatedly passing through the same layers without increasing model scale. This approach strengthens complex step-by-step reasoning capabilities in areas such as coding, computer use, and mathematics, and is even evaluated as a leap on par with the release of GPT-4. Concerns have emerged that unlike conventional reasoning AI, computation processes can be conducted in latent states without surfacing as natural language Chain of Thought (CoT), making it difficult to preemptively monitor anomalous behavior or security risks. \"Press WASD and AI generates video\"... Tencent unveils interactive world model 'H3-World' Tencent and its research team have unveiled 'H3-World', an action-controllable world model that generates video by manipulating characters and camera perspectives in real time via keyboard input. It converts keyboard inputs into short English instructions and applies directional attention masks to precisely control character and camera movements across time units. Instead of adding a complex control network, it leverages the language and motion understanding capabilities of the MiniMax H3 model, lightweight fine-tuning only 0.199% of total parameters using LoRA. Based on a single starting frame and key inputs, it generates an approximately 5.2-second video at 832x480 resolution, and has been open-sourced under the Apache 2.0 license. Changing AI's 'Memory Storage Order' Boosts Performance... High-Speed Weighted Attention Framework 'Falcon' Unveiled A joint research team has unveiled 'Falcon', a high-speed weighted attention framework that enhances long-context processing capabilities and performance by optimizing the memory update sequence in recurrent AI models. To resolve the mismatch between prediction timing and memory storage timing (temporal alignment errors) found in existing recurrent AI models, the memory update sequence was redesigned to a 'read-then-write' approach. Falcon-1, Falcon-2, Falcon-3, and dot-product variant models were proposed depending on normalization methods and update scopes, securing computational speed and memory efficiency by supporting recurrent, masked parallel, and chunk-parallel modes. In FineWeb-Edu-based experiments, Falcon-1.3 achieved better perplexity than Transformer, and demonstrated context retention significantly outperforming Transformer in variable-digit addition experiments. [Notice Board] Mindlogic to Provide Free ChatGPT and Gemini to High School Students, and Other News in Brief A brief roundup summarizing key business trends among major South Korean AI and tech companies, including winning educational welfare projects, participating in AI safety initiatives, adopting speech recognition solutions in customer centers, and entering global partnerships. Mindlogic was selected for the 9.5 billion KRW '2026 AI On-Ieum Project,' providing free multi-LLM-based AI services to 120,000 high school students. Aim Intelligence joined the SK Telecom consortium in the government-led 'AI for All' project to establish a safety system using AI red teaming and guardrail technologies. Selvas AI's speech recognition solution 'Selvas STT' was implemented at Bithumb's customer service center, reducing average handling time (AHT) by approximately 30%, while Doodlin joined the global company Workday's partner program. ePapyrus Surpasses 1 Billion Cumulative Downloads for AI Data Preprocessing Solution Driven by a surge in demand for LLMs and RAG, ePapyrus's PDF and data preprocessing library 'PyMuPDF' has surpassed 1 billion cumulative downloads globally. PyMuPDF has exceeded 1 billion cumulative downloads globally, with 83% occurring within the past year. By applying GNN-based layout analysis technology, it can convert documents into LLM-optimized formats up to 10 times faster than conventional vision AI models. Recently launched 'PyMuPDF Pro', which supports HWP and MS Office documents, and is supplying it to major global Big Tech companies and public institutions. Security Companies Joining Security-Specialized Model Initiative State: \"Roles Not Yet Distributed\" The Naver Cloud consortium has been finally selected for the Ministry of Science and ICT's project to develop a cybersecurity-specialized AI foundation model, and has begun establishing detailed development plans and distributing roles among major security companies. The Ministry of Science and ICT selected the Naver Cloud consortium, comprising 32 industry-academic-research institutions, as the final contractor for the project to develop a cybersecurity-specialized AI model. The selected consortium will receive 256 Nvidia B200 GPUs for 10 months, with subsequent support determined by the results of an interim evaluation at the 5-month mark. Detailed roles among participating security firms are still being finalized, but they will primarily provide core training data, such as threat intelligence and security appliance event logs, and handle model validation. US Government Backs OpenAI in AI Copyright Lawsuit: \"Restricting Training Stifles Innovation\" The US government has submitted an official brief to the court supporting OpenAI in the AI copyright lawsuit between The New York Times and OpenAI, viewing AI training as fair use. The Trump administration submitted a 20-page brief supporting OpenAI to the US District Court for the Southern District of New York regarding the copyright lawsuit filed by The New York Times against OpenAI. The US government determined that misinterpreting the fair use doctrine of copyright law to restrict LLM development could adversely affect US technological innovation and industrial competitiveness. While the government's brief is not directly binding on the court's ruling, it is drawing attention as the first instance of the US government expressing an official stance in an AI training copyright lawsuit. Agile Robots Brings Physical AI into the Real World, from Industrial Automation to Robot Training Data Agile Robots and its subsidiary Franka Robotics showcased their Physical AI vision combining industrial automation and bimanual robot training data at major European events. Agile Robots and its subsidiary Franka Robotics presented Physical AI research achievements and real-world deployment methods at major European events in Zurich and Bremen. They shared a vision of applying Physical AI to real-world industrial and research environments through intelligent robot technologies equipped with sensing, learning, and execution capabilities. They unveiled technologies spanning industrial automation and robot training data at events such as 'all about automation' in Zurich, Switzerland. [ZD SW Today] Kyobo DTS Launches AI Customer Service 'ConnectOne', and More Introduces recent developments among major domestic software and IT companies, including the launch of new AI-based customer service solutions, acquisition of technical competencies, platform unveilings, and milestone library downloads. Kyobo DTS, in partnership with Spectra, has launched 'ConnectOne', an integrated AI customer service tailored for small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) and small business owners. NDS demonstrated its full-cycle AI Transformation (AX) support capabilities by securing two AWS competency certifications in 'Generative AI Consulting Services' and 'Agentic AI Consulting Services'. VAIV Company, together with Lotte Members and Zero to One Partners, unveiled 'AI Twin Consumer', which combines purchase data with consumption contexts. ePapyrus's PDF processing library 'PyMuPDF' surpassed 1 billion cumulative global downloads, fueled by surging demand for AI data preprocessing."